The idea here is to use linearity of expectation by decomposing into the regions between aces, before the first ace, and after the last ace. By the symmetry of the problem, the expected sizes of the regions will be equal, and we can simply solve for the expectation.

$$$$

In particular, the $4$ aces in the deck will split it up into $5$ regions: cards before the first ace, between the first and second, and so on. Label these regions $1-5$, and let $X_i$ be the number of cards in region $i$. We are looking for $\mathbb{E}[X_1] + 1$. We must add in $1$ for the fact that $X_1$ is the number of cards that comes before the first ace, and we want the number of cards that must be observed, including the ace itself. Note that there are $52 - 4 = 48$ cards in the deck that are not aces. By the exchangeability of the different regions, as sampling without replacement is an exchangeable process and the labels of the regions are irrelevant, we have that $\mathbb{E}[X_1] = \dots = \mathbb{E}[X_5]$. Therefore, as there are $48$ cards to be assigned to the $5$ regions, we would expect $48/5$ cards per region on average. Therefore, $$\mathbb{E}[X_1] + 1 = \dfrac{53}{5} = 10.6$$.

Define $X_{i}$ to be the indicator random variable that is equal to 1 if card $i$ appears before the first Ace, and that is equal otherwise. Note that by card $i$ what we mean is each individual card. We could label them however we want, but for example, we could say that card 1 is the king of hearts, card 2 is the queen of spades, etc. Keep in mind that $X_{i}$ is $\textbf{not}$ representing the card in position $i$ of the deck, it is simply representing some card that we have decided to call card $i$. 

$$$$

If we then let $X$ be the number of cards that we must draw before seeing the first ace, but not including the ace card, then by the linearity of expectation and the definition of indicator random variables we have,

\begin{align*}
    \ev{X} = \ev{ \sum_{i=1}^{48} X_{i} } = \sum_{i=1}^{48} \ev{ X_{i} } = \sum_{i=1}^{48} \prob{ X_{i} = 1 }
\end{align*}

Note that the sum only goes up to 48 because there are 4 aces in the deck and so we have 48 non-ace cards. 

We now need to find $ \prob{ X_{i} = 1 } $. Since all permutations of cards are equally likely, the chance that card $i$ appears before the first ace is $\frac{1}{5}$. This is because there are $5!$ ways to arrange the 5 cards (whatever card is card $i$, and the 4 aces), and card $i$ appears before all of the aces in exactly 1 of these arrangements. We thus have,

\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=1}^{48} \prob{ X_{i} = 1 } = \sum_{i=1}^{48} \frac{1}{5} = \frac{48}{5}
\end{align*}

Note, however, that this is only the expectation of $X$, which we defined as the number of cards we need to draw before seeing the first ace, but not including the ace card. We thus need to add 1 to our answer, to count the ace card. The final result is thus, $\frac{48}{5} + 1 = \frac{53}{5} = 10.6$.

$$$$

Note: You may be wondering - why did we create our indicator variables in this way? Wouldn't it be more natural to say $X_{i}$ is 1 if the $ith$ card Scott pulls comes before the first ace? One certainly can do this, however, as you will see from the third solution, doing this makes the computation quite a bit more complicated since the $X_{i}$ now all have their own distribution. This is because the $\textbf{position}$ of a card in the deck clearly affects the probability that it appears before the first ace.


As mentioned in solution two, another way to do this (although infeasible by hand) would be to use the tail sum formula for non-negative integer-valued discrete random variables. Namely, for such random variables $X$, we have that $$\mathbb{E}[X] = \displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \mathbb{P}[X \geq k]$$ Defining our random variable $X$ as the number of cards needed to observe the first ace, in this scenario, the event $\{X \geq k\}$ means that there are no aces in the first $k-1$ cards. If there are no aces in the first $k-1$ cards, then the first ace must occur at the $k$th card or later, which is exactly the event $\{X \geq k\}$. Therefore, the probability that all $4$ aces lie in the last $52 - (k-1) = 53-k$ cards is $$\dfrac{\binom{53-k}{4}}{\binom{52}{4}}$$ To verify this formula is valid, plugging in $k = 1$ yields $1$ as the probability, which is what we expect since it must always take at least one turn to see the first ace. Note that the largest value of $k$ that $X$ can obtain is $49$, as this would be the case when the $4$ aces are the last $4$ cards of the deck, meaning that the first ace is the $49$th card drawn. Therefore, we could also compute $$\mathbb{E}[X] = \displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{49} \dfrac{\binom{53-k}{4}}{\binom{52}{4}} = 10.6$$ with significantly more effort than the approach above.

To set up this problem, let $R$ be the radial distance of the frog from the center. To compute the expectation, as we do not know the distribution of $R$ itself currently, we can use the tail probability formula for expected value $\ev{R} = \ctailsum{R}$. 

$$$$

In particular, the event $\{R \geq x\}$ means that the frog jumps to a point outside of the circle of radius $x$ with the same center as the lily pad. To compute $\prob{R \geq x}$, we need to find the probability the frog jumps to a point outside of the circle of radius $x$ with the same center as the lily pad. The area of the lily pad is $\pi$, while the area of the circle of radius $x$ is $\pi x^2$. This yields an area of $\pi(1-x^2)$ outside of the circle of radius $x$. Since the point is uniformly at random from the region, we can divide the area of the region of interest by the area of the total region, yielding $$\prob{R \geq x} = \dfrac{\pi(1-x^2)}{\pi} = 1-x^2$$ The values of $x$ that $R$ can take is $0 \leq x \leq 1$, as the lily pad is of radius $1$. Therefore, $$\ev{R} = \intab{0}{1} \prob{R \geq x} dx = \intab{0}{1} 1-x^2~dx = \dfrac{2}{3}$$ If we wanted to compute the precise PDF of $R$, we can use the tail probability to do this. In particular, since we have that $\prob{R \geq x} = 1-x^2$, we can obtain that the PDF of $R$ (for $0 \leq x \leq 1$) is $$f_R(x) = \dfrac{d}{dx}(\prob{R \leq x}) = \dfrac{d}{dx}(1 - \prob{R \geq x}) = \dfrac{d}{dx}(x^2) = 2x$$ This implies that $R \sim \bet{2}{1}$, which gives another way to compute the mean.

Our goal is to find the expected distance of the frog to the center of the lilly pad. Since we're trying to calculate an expected value, we can try to simply use the definition and calculate it directly. Let $R$ be the distance of the frog from the center of the lilly pad. By the definition of expected value we have,
\begin{equation*}
    \ev{R} = \int_{0}^{1} r f_{R}(r) dr
\end{equation*}
where $f_{R}(r)$ is the pdf of $R$. The bounds on the integral go from 0 to 1 because the Lilly pad has radius 1, and so the farthest away from the frog can be from the center is 1 meter.

$$$$

The problem now is that we need to find $f_{R}(r)$. To do this, we can first find the cdf, and then take the derivative to get the pdf. The cdf is,
\begin{equation*}
    \prob{R \leq r} = \frac{\pi r^{2}}{\pi} = r^{2}
\end{equation*}
which follows from the fact that the frog jumps uniformly at random on the lilly pad, so the chance that the frog lands in a circle of radius $r$ around the center is just the ratio of the area of that circle which is $\pi r^{2}$ to the area of the whole circle which is just $\pi$. Taking the derivative yields,
\begin{equation*}
    f_{R}(r) = 2r
\end{equation*}
so that the expected value is thus,
\begin{equation*}
    \ev{R} = \int_{0}^{1} r 2r dr = \int_{0}^{1} 2r^{2} dr = \frac{2}{3}
\end{equation*}

We want to find the expected cards after the first $2$ that we obtain strictly before the first ace. Given that this is a "first ace" type question, we should use the logic of the first ace paradigm to help us solve this more complicated problem. Our first task is to determine how many cards that aren't ranked $A$ nor $2$ show up between cards ranked $A$ or $2$ on average. This can be solved with a classic first ace method. We have $8$ dividers, which are all of the rank $A$ and $2$ cards. This is because we want to find the expected number of cards that appear between cards of rank $A$ or $2$, so we must treat both as dividers now. These dividers split the deck up into $9$ regions. By exchangeability, each of these regions contains the same number of cards in expectation. There are $52-8 = 44$ cards left that are not divider cards, so as the regions have equal size in expectation, the expected number of cards belonging to each region is $\dfrac{44}{9}$. Now, we need to find the expected number of regions that appear between the first $2$ and the first $A$. Using this, we will be able to find the total number of cards that appear on average between the first $2$ and first $A$.

$$$$

We now perform the first ace paradigm again on a new set of dividers. We want to find the expected number of $2$s that come before the first ace. This will give us the expected number of regions that we need to multiply by. This means that the aces are our dividers now and the only cards we want to consider placing between the aces are rank $2$ cards. Note that there are only $3$ cards of rank $2$ left, as one of them must have been drawn prior due to the fact that we condition on the first $2$ coming before the first $A$. The $4$ aces divide up our subset into $5$ regions of equal size in expectation by exchangeability. We have $3$ $2$s left, so there are on average $\dfrac{3}{5}$ $2$s per region. However, we already know that one $2$ appeared, so we must add $1$ to the number of regions that there will be. Therefore, our expected number of regions is $\dfrac{8}{5}$. However, we also need to account for the dividers, as we did not count for them previously when computing $\dfrac{44}{9}$. The average number of dividers that appear after the first $2$ is $\dfrac{3}{5}$, so we just add that quantity to the expression above, as we have only counted for the number of cards strictly between dividers up until this point.

$$$$

To get the total number of cards, we have to multiply the expected number of regions by the expected number of cards per region, and then add in the expected number of dividers that come before the first ace to get the total expected number of cards. Doing this, we obtain the answer $\dfrac{44}{9} \cdot \dfrac{8}{5} + \dfrac{3}{5} = \dfrac{379}{45}$.

We can solve this question by conditioning on the location of the first 2 and then use the law of total expectation. First, let $E_{i}$ be the event that the 1st 2 occurs at card $i$ for $i = 1,...,48$, let $B$ be the event that the first 2 appears before ace, and let $N$ be the number of cards between the first 2 and first ace. By the law of total expectation we have,

\begin{align*}
    \ev{N | B} &= \prob{E_{1} | B} \ev{N | B, E_{1}} + ... + \prob{E_{48} | B} \ev{N | B, E_{48}} \\
    &= \prob{E_{1} | B} \ev{N | B, E_{1}} + ... + \prob{E_{45} | B} \ev{N | B, E_{45}} \\
    &= \sum_{i =1}^{45} \prob{E_{i} | B} \ev{N | B, E_{i}} 
\end{align*}

which follows from the fact that $\prob{E_{46} | B} = \prob{E_{47} | B} = \prob{E_{48} | B} = 0$ since if the first 2 appears before the first ace, then the latest point that the first 2 can appear is card number 45, because otherwise there wouldn't be enough room to fit in the remaining 3 2's and 4 aces.

Our goal now will be to come up with formulas for $\prob{E_{i} | B} $ and $\ev{N | B, E_{i}}$ for $i= 1,...,45$. First, by Bayes rule we have,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{E_{i} | B} &= \frac{ \prob{E_{i},B} }{\prob{B}} = \frac{ \prob{E_{i},B} }{\frac{1}{2}} = 2 \prob{E_{i},B}
\end{align*}

which follows from the fact that by symmetry the first ace is equally likely to appear before the first 2 and vice-versa. We can then find $\prob{E_{i},B}$ via counting techniques, since each ordering of cards is equally likely. First, note that if the $ith$ card is the first time a 2 appears, then that means the previous $(i-1)$ cards could be anything except for a 2 or ace (keeping in mind that we also want our first 2 to appear before our first ace). Thus, of the possible 44 cards left, we need to pick $i-1$ of them, and these $i-1$ cards can be ordered in $(i-1)!$ ways, giving us a total of $\binom{44}{i-1}(i-1)!$ ways to place cards to the left of the first 2. To the right of the first 2, there are $(52 - i)$ cards which need to be ordered, which occurs in $(52-i)!$ ways. Last but not least, the $ith$ card itself can be any one of the four 2's available in the deck. Thus since there are $52!$ total orderings,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{E_{i},B} &= \frac{ \binom{44}{i-1}(i-1)!4(52-i)! }{52!} \\
    &= \frac{ 44!(52-i)!4 }{52!(45-i)!}
\end{align*}
and so $\prob{E_{i} | B} = 2 \prob{E_{i},B} = \frac{ 44!(52-i)!8 }{52!(45-i)!}$.

$$$$

To find an expression for $\ev{N | B, E_{i}}$ define the random variable $X_{j}$ for $j = i+1 ,.., 48$ so that $X_{j} = 1$ if the card in position $j$ in the deck appears after the first 2 and before the first ace, and $X_{j} = 0$ otherwise. Note that $j$ starts at $i+1$ since the $ith$ card is assumed to be a 2, and $j$ ends at 48 since the latest index that the first ace can appear is index 49. 

$$$$

Thus, $\sum_{j=i}^{48} X_{j}$ gives the number of cards in between the $ith$ card and the first ace. By the linearity of expectation, we thus have, 

\begin{align*}
    \ev{N | B, E_{i}} = \ev{ \sum_{j=i+1}^{48} X_{j} | B, E_{i} } &= \sum_{j=i+1}^{48} \ev{X_{j} | B, E_{i}} \\
    &= \sum_{j=i+1}^{48} \prob{X_{j} = 1 | B, E_{i}} \\
    &= \sum_{j=i+1}^{48} \frac{1}{5} \\
    &= \frac{1}{5}(48 - i)
\end{align*}

Where the second line follows from the definition of expected value of an indicator random variable, and the third line follows from the fact that any card has a $\frac{1}{5}$ chance of appearing before the first ace, since in any ordering of the $jth$ card and the 4 aces, there are 5 spots in which the $jth$ card can be, only one of which has the $jth$ card appearing before the aces.

Finally putting it altogether we have,

\begin{align*}
    \ev{N | B} = \sum_{i = 1}^{45} \frac{ 44! (52-i)! 8}{ (45-i)!52!}\frac{(48-i)}{5} = \frac{379}{45}
\end{align*}

Let $C(n)$ be the expected number of circles formed when there are $n$ noodles in the bowl. We want to try to derive a recurrence relation for $C(n)$, as it seems that $C(n)$ could be related to prior number of noodles in the bowl. Our answer is $C(60)$. When we have $n$ noodles in the bowl, we are first going to select one end uniformly at random from all of the $2n$ ends in the bowl. The probability that the end we selected is connected to the other end of the same noodle is just the probability that the other side we select is from the same noodle. There are $2n-1$ ends left to select from uniformly at random, and only $1$ of these ends belongs to the same noodle, so the probability we select that end is $\dfrac{1}{2n-1}$. Afterwards, there would only be $n-1$ noodles left in the bowl that have free ends to connect. Therefore, in this case, we obtain $1 + C(n-1)$ circles on average.

$$$$

In the event that the we don't select two ends that belong to the same noodle, we would tie the two ends we selected together, and now we would get an "elongated noodle" that would still have $2$ free ends left to connect. In this case, there are still now $n-1$ noodles left in the bowl, as the two noodles originally selected now combine to $1$ long noodle, but we didn't get an additional circle from it. Therefore, in this case, we obtain $C(n-1)$ circles on average.

$$$$

The above process hints to us that we should condition on whether or not the two ends we first select belong to the same noodle, In particular, with probability $\dfrac{1}{2n-1}$, we select two ends belonging to the same noodle, in which we would obtain $1 + C(n-1)$ circles on average from the process. Otherwise, with probability $1-\dfrac{1}{2n-1}$, we select two ends that don't belong to the same noodle, and would end with $C(n-1)$ noodles on average. This yields the recurrence relation $$C(n) = \dfrac{1}{2n-1}\left(1 + C(n-1)\right) + \left(1-\dfrac{1}{2n-1}\right)C(n-1) \iff C(n) = C(n-1) + \dfrac{1}{2n-1}$$ The base case for this is $C(1) = 1$, as with one noodle, you will always connect it back to itself. Therefore, by recursing backwards, $$C(n) = \dfrac{1}{2n-1} + \dfrac{1}{2(n-1)-1} + \dots + \dfrac{1}{3} + 1 = \sum_{i=1}^n \dfrac{1}{2i-1}$$ In particular, $C(60) \approx 3.03$.

We can also solve this problem using the indicator trick.

$$$$

Let $X$ be the total number of loops that are formed. Then, if we let $X_{i}$ be an indicator random variable that is equal to 1 if the $ith$ time we reach into bowl we create a circle and 0 otherwise. Note that by $ith$ time, we mean the $ith$ time that we grab two noodle ends. Then, the total number of circles that will be created is,
\begin{align*}
    X = \sum_{i=1}^{n} X_{i}
\end{align*}
and then by the linearity of expectation and properties of indicator random variables we will have,
%
\begin{align*}
    \ev{X} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \ev{ X_{i} } = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \prob{X_{i} = 1}
\end{align*}
and our task will now be to determine $\prob{X_{i} = 1}$ for each $i$. Unlike other cases in which we've used the indicator trick, this will be a little bit more complicated to calculate. The reason for this is that the probability will change with each $i$. 

$$$$

So, how do we find $\prob{X_{i} = 1}$? If we're at the $ith$ draw of noodles, then that means we've pulled from the bowl $i-1$ times, which means we've tied together $2(i-1)$ noodle ends (remember, at each draw of noodles we grab two ends and tie them together!). This means that when we're at the $ith$ draw of noodles, there are currently $2n - 2(i-1) = 2(n-i+1)$ noodle ends in the bowl to choose from. Since every noodle end will form a circle if we select exactly one other noodle end, there are $(n-i+1)$ ways that we can select two noodle ends that will form a circle.

$$$$

Now, we just need to find the total number of ways we can select two noodle ends. As mentioned, there are $2(n-i-1)$ noodle ends currently in the ramen bowl, so there are,
\begin{align*}
    \binom{2(n-i+1)}{2} &= \frac{ \left( 2(n-i+1) \right)!}{2!\left( 2(n-i+1) - 2 \right)! } \\
    &= \frac{2(n-i+1)\left( 2(n-i+1)-1\right)}{2!} \\
    &= (n-i+1)\left( 2(n-i+1)-1\right)
\end{align*}
ways to pick two noodle ends. Putting these two terms together yields,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{X_{i} = 1} &= \frac{(n-i+1) }{(n-i+1)\left( 2(n-i+1)-1\right)} \\
    &= \frac{1}{ 2(n-i+1)-1 } \\
    &= \frac{1}{ 2n-2i+1 }
\end{align*}

Finally taking this probability and plugging it in to the formula for the expected value we get,
\begin{align*}
    \ev{X} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{1}{ 2n-2i+1 }
\end{align*}
and plugging in $n=60$ and using a calculator yields,
\begin{align*}
    \ev{X} \approx 3.03
\end{align*}

In this question, it is important to note the edge case of the dice showing the same values on the first roll or not. This is because that there are $2$ permutations of the values between the dice if they are distinct, while there is only $1$ permutation if they are the same. Therefore, it is helpful to condition on whether or not the dice show the same value. Let $S_1$ be the event that the two dice show the same value on the first roll and $M$ be the event that the values of the dice on the second roll match those of the first roll. We want $\prob{M}$. By the law of total probability and conditioning on $S_1$ we have that

\begin{align*}
\prob{M} = \prob{M \mid S_1}\prob{S_1} + \prob{M \mid S_1^c}\prob{S_1^c}
\end{align*}

First, let's compute $\prob{S_1}$. This is the probability that on the first roll, the two dice show the same value. Since the two dice are identical and fair, we can fix the value of the first die arbitrarily. Once we do so, exactly $1$ of the $6$ possible outcomes for the second die will match that of the first die, so $\prob{S_1} = \dfrac{1}{6}$, meaning $\prob{S_1^c} = 1 - \dfrac{1}{6} = \dfrac{5}{6}$. Now, as stated at the beginning of this question, if the values of the dice match, say $(a,a)$, then the values $(a,a)$ must appear on the two dice in the second roll, which is $1$ of $36$ equally likely outcomes. Therefore, $\prob{M \mid S_1} = \dfrac{1}{36}$. On the other hand, if the two values do not match, say they are $(a,b)$ with $a \neq b$. Then either $(a,b)$ or $(b,a)$ between the two dice rolls will give us the same set of values, so $\prob{M \mid S_1^c} = \dfrac{2}{36}$. 

$$$$

Plugging all of this information into the law of total probability and summing gives an answer of $\dfrac{11}{216}$.

The first task is to convert this problem into a probabilistic question. Let $T$ and $S$ be the number of minutes after $12$ PM that the TA and the student show up, respectively. From the question, $T \sim \text{Unif}(30,60)$ and $S \sim \text{Unif}(0,60)$. The event that the meeting occurs is the just $\{|T-S| \leq 10\}$, as they must differ by at most $10$ minutes. To compute $\prob{|T-S| \leq 10}$, we should draw out the region in the plane to see what we are working with. Since $S$ and $T$ are both uniform random variables, we can take ratios of areas to compute probabilities. 

$$$$

If you put $T$ on the $x$-axis and $S$ on the $y$-axis, we have a tall rectangle as our support. When drawing the two lines corresponding to $|T-S| \leq 10$, you will notice that it is easier to calculate the probability of the complement, as those are regions whose areas are simpler to compute via geometry. The complement consists of one trapezoid and one triangle. It is easy enough to use some basic algebra to note that the slope is 1, so the line will go as far vertically as it does horizontally. You will find that the base of the trapezoid is of length $30$ (which is just the base of the rectangle), and the two heights are of lengths $20$ and $50$, so its area is $\frac{1}{2} \cdot 30 (20 + 50) = 1050$. The two sides of the triangle are length $20$ each, so the area is $1/2 \cdot 20^2 = 200$. Thus, the probability of the complement is $1250/1800 = 25/36$. Thus, the probability in the question is $11/36$.

The previous solution leveraged the fact that probabilities involving uniformly distributed random variables can be calculated by calculating the ratio of different areas, which ideally one would determine by first drawing a picture. But what if we don't feel like drawing a picture, or what if our interviewer decides to be a little angry and says we can't do that? In working through this solution, we'll see exactly why relying on areas is so desirable - this solution is quite a bit longer than the previous!

$$$$

As mentioned in the previous solution, we need to compute, $\prob{ | T - S | \leq 10}$.

$$$$

Since the probability statement involves the expression $|T-S|$, whose expression depends on whether or not $T > S$ or vice versa, we'll apply the law of total probability and condition on the event $T \geq S$. Along with using Bayes rule, this gives,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{ | T - S | \leq 10} &= \prob{ T - S \leq 10 | T \geq S } \prob{T \geq S} + \prob{ S - T \leq 10 | T < S} \prob{T < S}\\
    &= \prob{ T \leq 10 + S | T \geq S } \prob{T \geq S} + \prob{ S  \leq 10 + T | T < S} \prob{T < S} \\
    &= \dfrac{ \prob{ T \leq 10 + S , T \geq S} }{\prob{T \geq S}} \prob{T \geq S } + \dfrac{ \prob{ S \leq 10 + T , T < S} }{\prob{T <S}} \prob{T < S} \\
    &= \prob{ T \leq 10 + S , T \geq S } + \prob{ S  \leq 10 + T , T < S} 
\end{align*}
And now, we just need to evaluate each of these probabilities, which we can do via integration. First, let's keep in mind that since $S \sim U(0,60)$ and $T \sim U(30,60)$ they have probability density functions,
\begin{align*}
    f_{S}(s) = \frac{1}{60} \Indicator{ 0 \leq s \leq 60} \: \: \text{and} \: \: f_{T}(t) = \frac{1}{30} \Indicator{ 30 \leq t \leq 60},
\end{align*}
respectively. Starting with the probability on the left we have,

\begin{align*}
    \prob{ T \leq 10 + S , T \geq S } &= \prob{ T - 10 \leq S , T \geq S} \\
    &= \int_{30}^{60} \int_{ t-10 }^{t} \frac{1}{30} \Indicator{ 30 \leq t \leq 60} \frac{1}{60} \Indicator{ 0 \leq s \leq 60} ds dt \\
    &= \int_{30}^{60} \int_{ t-10 }^{t} \frac{1}{30} \frac{1}{60}  ds dt \\
    &= \frac{1}{6}
\end{align*}
where the third equality followed from the fact that both indicators were always equal to 1, as $t$ was clearly always between 30 and 60, and $t-10$ was always at least $30 - 10 = 20$, and $t$ was always at most 60, so that $s$ was always between 0 and 60.

Moving on to the probability on the right-hand side we have,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{ S  \leq 10 + T , T < S} &= \int_{30}^{60} \int_{t}^{\text{min}(10 + t, 60)} \frac{1}{30} \Indicator{ 30 \leq t \leq 60} \frac{1}{60} \Indicator{ 0 \leq s \leq 60} ds dt \\
    &= \int_{30}^{60} \int_{t}^{\text{min}(10 + t, 60)} \frac{1}{30} \frac{1}{60}  ds dt
\end{align*}
The upper bound in the inner integral follows from the fact that since $S \sim U(0,60)$, it can't be larger than 60, but $10 + t > 60$ if $t > 50$, and $t$ can range from 30 to 60 since $T \sim U(30,60)$. The second equality follows since the indicators are always equal to 1, since their conditions are always satisfied. Note also that, 
\begin{align*}
    \text{min}(10 + t, 60) &= \begin{cases}
        60 & \text{if } t \geq 50 \\
        10 + t & \text{if } t < 50 
    \end{cases} \\
\end{align*}
So that the integral is equal to,
\begin{align*}
    \int_{30}^{60} \int_{t}^{\text{min}(10 + t, 60)} \frac{1}{30} \frac{1}{60} ds dt &= \int_{30}^{50} \int_{t}^{10+t} \frac{1}{30} \frac{1}{60} ds dt + \int_{50}^{60} \int_{t}^{60} \frac{1}{30} \frac{1}{60} ds dt \\
    &= \frac{5}{6}
\end{align*}

Returning back to our original expression and putting everything back together we get,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{ | T - S | \leq 10} &= \frac{1}{6} + \frac{5}{36} = \frac{11}{36}
\end{align*}
Which is the final answer.

$$$$

