The approach here is to use the tail integral formula $\ev{X} = \ctailsum{X}$ on $M_n$. The event $\{M_n \geq x\}$ isn't very conducive to work with, as $M_n \geq x$ does not tell us anything about all of the individual $X_i$ random variables. However, we do know that $M_n < x$ tells us that each of the $X_i < x$, as if the largest of the $n$ is less than $x$, all of the random variables must also be less than $x$. Thus, we consider the complement. Namely, $$\prob{M_n \geq x} = 1 - \prob{M_n < x} = 1 - \prob{\max(X_1,\dots,X_n) < x} =1 - \prob{X_1 < x,\dots, X_n < x}$$ Now, note that $X_1,\dots, X_n$ are independent, so the last term splits becomes $1 - \prob{X_1 < x}\dots \prob{X_n < x}$. Since each of $X_1,\dots, X_n$ are also identically distributed, all of the probability statements are the same, so this further gets reduced to $1 - (\prob{X_1 < x})^n$. We restrict ourselves to $a < x < b$, as the support of $X_1$ is only $(a,b)$. The event $\{X_1 < x\}$ is of length $x-a$ inside an interval of length $b-a$. Since $X_1$ is uniform on this interval, we can take ratios of lengths to compute probabilities. Namely, this implies $\prob{X_1 < x} = \dfrac{x-a}{b-a}$. Therefore, for $a < x < b$, $$\prob{M_n \geq x} = 1 - \left(\dfrac{x-a}{b-a}\right)^n$$ We also have that $\prob{X_1 > x} = 1$ for $x < a$, as we know that the support of $X$ is $(a,b)$, so it is always larger than $a$. Plugging this in, $$\ev{M_n} = \ctailsum{M_n} = \int_0^a 1dx + \int_a^{b} 1 - \left(\dfrac{x-a}{b-a}\right)^ndx = a + (b-a) - \dfrac{1}{(b-a)^n}\int_a^b (x-a)^ndx = a + \dfrac{(b-a)n}{n+1}$$ This answer makes intuitive sense. As the $n$ random variables are uniformly distributed on the interval $(a,b)$, the spacings between consecutive random variables should be exchangeable i.e. follow the same distribution. This encapsulates the idea that, on average, each of the spacings should be the same, as we don't expect any to be closer or further away from each other. These $n$ uniform random variables split up the interval $(a,b)$ into $n+1$ segments, which would each have an average length of $\frac{b-a}{n+1}$ by the above. Note that the interval starts at $a$, so we need to add $a$ to our expected value as well to account for the starting point. These segments only account for the distance from the previous order statistic. There are $n$ such segments before the $n$th order statistic, so the average distance from the LHS of the interval is $\frac{b-a}{n+1} \cdot n$. Adding these together, the result is that $\ev{M_n} = a + \frac{(b-a)n}{n+1}$, which is exactly the answer we obtained above. With the specified values, the answer is $14$.

Since we're trying to calculate an expected value, we can try to simply use the definition and calculate it directly. Let $Z = \text{max}(X_{1},...,X_{n})$. By the definition of expected value we have,
\begin{equation*}
    \ev{Z} = \int_{a}^{b} z f_{Z}(z) dz
\end{equation*}
where $f_{Z}(z)$ is the pdf of $Z$. The bounds on the integral come from the fact that since each $X_{i} \sim \unif{a}{b}$ then their maximum must also be between $a$ and $b$. To find $f_{Z}(z)$ we will take the familiar approach of finding $F_{Z}(z)$, the cdf, and then differentiating. By definition this gives,

\begin{align*}
    F_{Z}(z) &= \prob{ Z < z} \\
    &= \prob{ \text{max}(X_{1},...,X_{n}) < z} \\
    &= \prob{ X_{1} < z, ..., X_{n} < z} \\
    &= \prod_{i=1}^{n} \prob{X_{i} < z} \\
    &= \prob{X_{1} < z}^{n} \\
    &= \left( \frac{z - a}{b - a} \right)^{n}
\end{align*}
%
Where the third equality follows from the fact that if the maximum of the $X_{i}$ is less than some value $z$, then all of the $X_{i}$ must be less than that value, the fourth equality follows from the fact that each $X_{i}$ is independent, the fifth from the fact that each $X_{i}$ is identically distributed, and the last equality from the fact that $X_{1} \sim \unif{a}{b}$. Differentiating with respect to $z$ yields,

\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dz} z^{n} &= n \left( \frac{z-a}{b - a} \right)^{n-1} \frac{1}{b-a}
\end{align*}

The expected value will thus be,
\begin{align*}
    \ev{Z} &= \int_{a}^{b} z n \left( \frac{z-a}{b - a} \right)^{n-1} \frac{1}{b-a} dz \\
    &= \frac{n}{b-a} \int_{a}^{b} z \left( \frac{z-a}{b - a} \right)^{n-1} dz \\
    &= \frac{n}{(b-a)^{n}} \int_{a}^{b} z (z-a)^{n-1} dz \\
    &= \frac{n}{(b-a)^{n}} \int_{0}^{b - a} (u+a) u^{n-1} du \\
    &= \frac{n}{(b-a)^{n}} \left( \int_{0}^{b - a} u^{n} du + \int_{0}^{b - a} a u^{n-1} du \right) \\
    &= \frac{n}{(b-a)^{n}} \left( \frac{u^{n+1}}{n+1} \bigg|_{0}^{b-a} + a\frac{u^{n}}{n} \bigg|_{0}^{b-a} du \right) \\
    &= \frac{n}{(b-a)^{n}} \left( \frac{(b-a)^{n+1}}{n+1} + \frac{a (b-a)^{n}}{n}  \right) \\
\end{align*}
Where we used the $u-$substitution, $u = z - a$, to solve the integral. Plugging in the provided numbers from the question gives,
\begin{equation*}
    \ev{Z} = \frac{9}{(15-5)^{9}} \left( \frac{(15- 5)^{9+1}}{9+1} + \frac{5 (15-5)^{9}}{9}  \right) = 14
\end{equation*}

