\end{equation*}

Our task is now to find these probabilities. To find $\prob{E | L_{14}}$, note that if we know Lilly got card $14$, then in order for Lewis to only get even numbered cards he had to have picked his $5$ cards from the set $\{2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12\}$. Since there are only $14$ cards available (remember that Lilly has card $14$, leaving $15 - 1 = 14$ cards for Lewis), the probability that all of Lewis $5$ cards come from this set is $\frac{6}{14} \frac{5}{13} \frac{4}{12} \frac{3}{11} \frac{2}{10} = \frac{3}{1001}$. Alternatively, we can calculate this probability by noting that there are $\binom{14}{5} = 2002$ ways for Lewis to choose 5 cards from the 14 available cards, and $\binom{6}{5} = 6$ ways for him to choose $5$ cards from the $6$ remaining even cards, and $\frac{6}{2002} = \frac{3}{1001}$. Thus, $\prob{E | L_{14}} = \frac{3}{1001}$.

