Since we want to find the pdf of $Y$, we will proceed by first finding the cdf of $Y$. The cdf of $Y$ is $\prob{Y \leq z} = \prob{X_{1}X_{2}X_{3}X_{4} \leq z}$, but this looks difficult to deal with. Our next step will be to try to turn the product into something more manageable. Since logarithms turn multiplication into addition, we will take the natural log of both sides. For reasons that will become clear shortly, we will also multiply each side by $-1$. Performing these operations gives,

\begin{align*}
    \prob{Y \leq z} = \prob{X_{1}X_{2}X_{3}X_{4} \leq z} = \prob{-\text{log}(X_{1}) + -\text{log}(X_{2}) + -\text{log}(X_{3}) + -\text{log}(X_{4}) \geq -\text{log}(z)}
\end{align*}

Let's now inspect, say, $-\text{log}(X_{1})$. What is the distribution of this random variable? We can find it by considering it's cdf, so that for $w \in [0, \infty)$,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{ -\text{log}(X_{1}) \leq w } &= \prob{ \text{log}(X_{1}) \geq -w } \\
    &= \prob{ X_{1} \geq e^{-w} } \\
    &= 1 - \prob{ X_{1} \leq e^{-w} } \\
    &= 1 - e^{-w} \\
\end{align*}

where the last equality follows from the fact that $X_{1}$ is uniform. Differentiating with respect to $w$ in order to get the pdf yields, $f_{-\text{log}(X_{1})}(w) = e^{-w}$, which can be recognized as the pdf of an exponential random variable with $\lambda = 1$. Thus we can write the cdf of $Y$ as,
\begin{align*}
    \prob{Y \leq z} &= \prob{-\text{log}(X_{1}) + -\text{log}(X_{2}) + -\text{log}(X_{3}) + -\text{log}(X_{4}) \geq -\text{log}(z)} \\
    &= \prob{G \geq -\text{log}(z)} \\
    &= \prob{ e^{-G} \leq z }\\
\end{align*}

Where $G$ is a $\gam{4}{1}$ random variable, since $\sum_{i=1}^{4} -\text{log}X_{i}$ is a sum of 4 independent $\exponential{1}$ random variables. Since the pdf of a random variable is simply the derivative of the cdf, the above also gives us,
\begin{align*}
    f_{Y}(z) &= \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \prob{Y \leq z} \\
    &= \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \prob{ e^{-G} \leq z } \\
    &= f_{e^{-G}}(z)
\end{align*}

Thus, if we find the pdf of $e^{-G}$, we will have also found the pdf of $Y$, since their pdfs are equivalent. Since $h(G) = e^{-G}$ is a strictly decreasing and differentiable function of $G$, we can find the pdf of $e^{-G}$ using the typical method of transformations. If we let $Z = h(G) = e^{-G}$, then for $z$ such that $-\text{log}(z) \in (0, \infty)$ we have,
\begin{align*}
    f_{e^{-G}}(z) = f_{Z}(z) &= f_{G}( h^{-1}(G) ) \big| \frac{\partial}{\partial z} h^{-1}(G) \big| \\
    &= \frac{1}{\Gamma(4)} (-\text{log}(z))^{3}e^{\text{log}(z)} \frac{1}{z} \\
    &= \frac{1}{\Gamma(4)} (-\text{log}(z))^{3}
 \end{align*}
Note that the condition $-\text{log}(z) \in (0, \infty)$ follows from the fact that the Gamma distribution is defined only on $(0, \infty)$. Since, the method of transformations requires us to evaluate $f_{G}( h^{-1}(G) ) = f_{G}( -\log(z) )$, we must have that $-\text{log}(z) \in (0, \infty)$. 

Plugging in $z = 0.5$ yields the answer $0.06$.

