To find $f_{Z}(z)$ we will take the approach of finding $F_{Z}(z)$, the cdf, and then differentiating. By definition this gives,

\begin{align*}
    F_{Z}(z) &= \prob{ Z < z} \\
    &= \prob{ \text{max}(X_{1},...,X_{n}) < z} \\
    &= \prob{ X_{1} < z, ..., X_{n} < z} \\
    &= \prod_{i=1}^{n} \prob{X_{i} < z} \\
    &= \prob{X_{1} < z}^{n} \\
    &= z^{n}
\end{align*}

Where the third equality follows from the fact that if the maximum of the $X_{i}$ is less than some value $z$, then all of the $X_{i}$ must be less than that value, the fourth equality follows from the fact that each $X_{i}$ is independent, the fifth from the fact that each $X_{i}$ is identically distributed, and the last equality from the fact that $X_{1} \sim \unif{0}{1}$. Differentiating with respect to $z$ yields,

\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dz} z^{n} &= nz^{n-1}
\end{align*}
Thus, evaluating at $z = \frac{1}{2}$ and $n = 5$ gives, $5 (\frac{1}{2})^{4} = \frac{5}{16}$

To find $f_{W}(w)$ we will take the approach of finding $F_{W}(w)$, the cdf, and then differentiating. By definition this gives,

\begin{align*}
    F_{W}(w) &= \prob{ W < w} \\
    &= \prob{ \text{min}(X_{1},...,X_{n}) < w} \\
    &= 1 - \prob{ \text{min}(X_{1},...,X_{n}) > w} \\
    &= 1 - \prob{ X_{1} > w, ..., X_{n} > w} \\
    &= 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{n} \prob{X_{i} > w} \\
    &= 1 - \prob{X_{1} > w}^{n} \\
    &= 1 - (1 - w)^{n}
\end{align*}

Where the fourth equality follows from the fact that if the minimum of the $X_{i}$ is greater than some value $z$, then all of the $X_{i}$ must be greater than that value, the fifth equality follows from the fact that each $X_{i}$ is independent, the sixth from the fact that each $X_{i}$ is identically distributed, and the last equality from the fact that $X_{1} \sim \unif{0}{1}$. Differentiating with respect to $2$ yields,

\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dw} \big[ 1 - (1 - w)^{n} \big] &= n(1-w)^{n-1}
\end{align*}
Thus, evaluating at $w = \frac{1}{2}$ and $n = 5$ gives, $5 (1-\frac{1}{2})^{4} = \frac{5}{16}$

To find $f_{Z,W}(z,w)$ we will take the familiar approach of finding $F_{Z,W}(z,w)$, the cdf, and then differentiating. However, the complication we encounter is that it's not immediately clear what to do once we've written

\begin{align*}
    F_{Z,W}(z,w) = \prob{Z \leq z, W \leq w}
\end{align*}

We can note, however, that the law of total probability gives us the following relation.

\begin{align*}
    \prob{Z \leq z} = \prob{Z \leq z, W \leq w} + \prob{Z \leq z, W > w}
\end{align*}

The left hand term, $\prob{Z \leq z}$, is equal to $z^{n}$ as seen from the problem "Max of $n$ uniforms". The right hand term, $\prob{Z \leq z, W > w}$ is equal to 0 if $w > z$, and if $w \leq z$ is equal to $(w-z)^{n}$ since

\begin{align*}
    \prob{Z \leq z, W > w} &= \prob{ w < X_{1} \leq z, ..., w < X_{n} \leq z} \\
    &= \prod_{i=1}^{n} \prob{ w < X_{i} \leq z} \\
    &= (z-w)^{n}
\end{align*}

The first equality follows from the fact that if the maximum of the $X_{i}$ is less than z, the minimum of the $X_{i}$ is greater than $w$, and $z \geq w$, then each $X_{i}$ must be between $z$ and $w$. The second follows from the fact the $X_{i}$ are independent, and the third from the fact that they're $\unif{0}{1}$. 

Putting this together gives

\begin{align*}
    z^{n} &= \prob{Z \leq z, W \leq w} + (z-w)^{n} \\
    \rightarrow \prob{Z \leq z, W \leq w} &= z^{n} - (z-w)^{n}
\end{align*}

and differentiating with respect to $z$ and $w$ yields,

\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dw}\frac{d}{dz} \big\{ z^{n} - (z-w)^{n} \big\} &= \frac{d}{dw}\big\{ nz^{n-1} - n(z-w)^{n-1} \big\} \\
    &= (n-1)n(z-w)^{n-2}
\end{align*}

Evaluating this at $z = 0.7, w = 0.5$, and $n = 5$ gives the final answer as $(5-1)5(.7-.5)^{5-2} = \frac{4}{25}$.

The straightforward approach to this problem is to condition on Lewis first roll and then use the law of total expectation, where we continue to condition on the result of subsequent rolls. For small $n$, this approach is feasible, but will quickly get unwieldy as $n$ grows.

Instead, let $E_{k}\left(n\right)$ denote the expected number of rolls needed to get a cumulative sum of at least $n$, assuming that the current sum is $k$. Our goal is to thus find $E_{0}\left(n\right)$, since Lewis is starting off with a total sum of 0 before he starts rolling. Let $R_{i}$ be the event that Lewis' next roll takes value $i$. Conditioning on the outcome of Lewis' next roll and using the law of total expectation yields,

\begin{align*}
    E_{k}\left(n\right) &= \prob{R_{1}}\left( 1 + E_{k+1}\left(n\right)\right) + ... + \prob{R_{n}}\left( 1 + E_{k+n}\left(n\right)\right) \\
    &= 1 + \frac{1}{n}E_{k+1}\left(n\right) + ... + \frac{1}{n}E_{k+n}\left(n\right) \\
    &= 1 + \frac{1}{n}E_{k+1}\left(n\right) + ... + \frac{1}{n}E_{n-1}\left(n\right)
\end{align*}

Which follows from the fact that the dice is fair, and that if Lewis' sum is at least $n$, he doesn't need to roll anymore so that $E_{k}\left(n\right) = 0$ for $k \geq n$. This looks difficult to deal with, however, if we analyze the terms a pattern will emerge. 

First, note that $E_{n-1}\left(n\right) = 1$, since if Lewis is only $1$ away from $n$, then no matter what he rolls next, his cumulative sum of rolls will be at least $n$. Let's now analyze $E_{n-2}\left(n\right)$. We have

\begin{align*}
    E_{n-2}\left(n\right) &= 1 + \frac{1}{n} E_{n-1} \\
    &= \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)
\end{align*}

Looking at $E_{n-3}\left(n\right)$ yields

\begin{align*}
    E_{n-3}\left(n\right) &= 1 + \frac{1}{n} E_{n-2} + \frac{1}{n} E_{n-1} \\
    &= 1 + \frac{1}{n}\left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right) + \frac{1}{n}\left(1\right) \\
    &= 1 + \frac{2}{n} + \frac{1}{n^{2}}
\end{align*}

Looking at one more term gives

\begin{align*}
    E_{n-4}\left(n\right) &= 1 + \frac{1}{n} E_{n-3} + \frac{1}{n} E_{n-2} + \frac{1}{n} E_{n-1} \\
    &= 1 + \frac{1}{n}\left(1 + \frac{2}{n} + \frac{1}{n^{2}}\right) +  \frac{2}{n} + \frac{1}{n^{2}}\\
    &= 1 + \frac{1}{n} + \frac{2}{n^{2}} + \frac{1}{n^{3}} + \frac{2}{n} + \frac{1}{n^{2}} \\
    &= 1 + \frac{3}{n} + \frac{3}{n^{2}} + \frac{1}{n^{3}} \\
    &= \frac{ \binom{3}{0}}{n^{0}} + \frac{ \binom{3}{1} }{n^{1}} + \frac{ \binom{3}{2}}{n^{2}} + \frac{ \binom{3}{3} }{n^{3}} \\
    &= \sum_{i=0}^{3} \frac{ \binom{3}{i}}{n^{i}}
\end{align*}

By induction, one can show that

\begin{align*}
    E_{n-k}\left(n\right) = \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \frac{ \binom{k-1}{i}}{n^{i}}
\end{align*}

Solving this sum by hand at first appears to be impossible, but we can use the binomial theorem by noting

\begin{align*}
    E_{n-k}\left(n\right) &= \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \frac{ \binom{k-1}{i}}{n^{i}} \\
    &= \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} \binom{k-1}{i} \left(\frac{1}{n}\right)^{i}\left(1\right)^{k-1-i} \\
    &= \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^{k-1}
\end{align*}

Since Lewis begins with a sum of $0$, we are interested in $E_{0}\left(n\right)$, which is equal to

\begin{align*}
    E_{0}\left(n\right) = E_{n - n}\left(n\right) = \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^{n-1}
\end{align*}

When $n = 6$, this yields $\left(1 + \frac{1}{6}\right)^{5} = \frac{16807}{7776}$.

