\begin{align*}
    \ev{N} &= \prob{R_{1}} \ev{N |R_{1}} + ... + \prob{R_{6}} \ev{N | R_{6}} \\
    &= \frac{5}{6}(1 + \ev{N}) + \frac{1}{6}(1 + \ev{N | R_{4} }) \\
    &= 1 + \frac{5}{6}\ev{N} + \frac{1}{6}\ev{N | R_{4}} \\
\end{align*}

To find $\ev{N | R_{4}}$ note that there are three possibilities. Either we roll a 5, in which case we are done, we roll a 4 in which case we are done if our next roll is a 5, or we roll a 1,2,3, or 6, in which case we effectively have to start over. Thus, $\ev{N | R_{4}} = \frac{1}{6}(1) + \frac{1}{6}\ev{N|R_{4}} + \frac{4}{6}(1 + \ev{N})$ and solving for $\ev{N | R_{4}}$ gives us, $\ev{N | R_{4}} = \frac{4}{5}\ev{N} + \frac{6}{5}$. Thus we have,

\begin{align*}
