Let the two cutpoints that Lewis' choose be denoted as $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$, where $X_{1}$ is the cutpoint that is to the left of the midpoint of the piece of wood and $X_{2}$ is the cutpoint that is to the right of the midpoint of the piece of wood. Since they are chosen uniformly at random we have $X_{1} \sim \unif{0}{\frac{1}{2}}$ and $X_{2} \sim \unif{\frac{1}{2}}{1}$. We are thus interested in finding $\prob{ X_{2} - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3}}$. Since we have two sources of randomness here, let's condition on one of them and use the law of total probability. This yields

\begin{align*}
    \prob{ X_{2} - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3}} &= \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} \prob{ X_{2} - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3} | X_{1} = x} f_{X_{1}}(x) dx \\
